d pods. The next step is to check the metrics again for user-service. Wait, the initial `get_metrics` query for `up{job="user-service"}` returned an empty result, indicating that the user-service might not be running or not being scraped by Prometheus. But the deployment and pod status show it\'s running. This discrepancy suggests a possible issue with the Prometheus metrics endpoint or the service configuration. Alternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t exposing the metrics port. The next step would be to check the service configuration for user-service to see if it\'s correctly set up to expose metrics. But the available tools don\'t include a direct way to check service configurations. Alternatively, using `kubectl describe` on the user-service pod to check for any errors in the container logs. Wait, the `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` can be used with `kubectl logs` to get the logs of the pod. So the next step is to retrieve the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors causing it to not be healthy. That would help identify the root cause. So the tool call should be `kubectl logs user-service-<pod-name> -n social-network` but since the exact pod name isn\'t known, maybe the user needs to first get the pod name. However, the `kubectl get pods` command lists all pods, so the user can identify the user-service pod\'s name. But the previous `kubectl get pods` output didn\'t show the pod names, only the status. Wait, the output from `kubectl get pods -n social-network` includes the pod names. For example, the user-service deployment\'s pod might be named something like user-service-<hash>. So the next step is to get the logs of the user-service pod. Therefore, the correct tool call would be to use `kubectl logs` on the user-service pod. But since the exact pod name isn\'t provided in the previous output, the user might need to first get the pod name. However, the `kubectl get pods` command in the previous response didn\'t list the pod names, only th