pods` and saw the list. So the next step is to use `kubectl logs` on the user-service pod. But without the exact pod name, the user might need to use the deployment name. Alternatively, the user can use `kubectl logs` with the deployment name, but that\'s not directly possible. Therefore, the correct approach is to first get the pod name and then retrieve its logs. However, the available tools only allow read-only commands, so the user can use `kubectl logs` with the pod name. Therefore, the next tool call should be `kubectl logs user-service-<pod-name> -n social-network`. But since the exact pod name isn\'t known, the user might need to first get the pod name. However, the previous `kubectl get pods` output might have listed the pod names. For example, if the user-service deployment has a pod named user-service-abc123, then the logs command would be `kubectl logs user-service-abc123 -n social-network`. Therefore, the next step is to retrieve the logs of the user-service pod to check for any errors. Hence, the tool call should be `kubectl logs user-service-<pod-name> -n social-network`. But since the exact pod name isn\'t provided, the user might need to first get the pod name. However, the previous `kubectl get pods` output might have included the pod names. Therefore, the user should use the pod name from that list. So the final tool call is to get the logs of the user-service pod.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-<pod-name> -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b1d7dc5293233ca3', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1553, 'prompt_tokens': 3420, 'total_tokens': 4973, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'open