'value\': \'user-service\'}, {\'key\': \'ip\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'127.0.0.1\'}, {\'key\': \'opencensus.exporterversion\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'Jaeger-C++-0.4.2\'}]}}, \'warnings\': None}]", 572)', id='7fa467cf-2bd5-447d-b54e-042cbcf052e1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8d0db23dfc98211a'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8dd7741-99c1-4a64-a09d-2628e8d4a407'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Social Network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the services using get_services, looked at deployments with kubectl, checked pod statuses, and tried to get logs but faced an error. Then they used get_traces on the user-service and found some traces with warnings about invalid parent span IDs.\n\nHmm, the traces show warnings about invalid parent span IDs, which might indicate a problem with the tracing setup or some issues in the service\'s dependencies. Since the user-service is part of the social network, maybe there\'s a problem with how it\'s communicating with other services via Thrift RPCs. The invalid parent span IDs could mean that the service isn\'t properly tracking or linking spans across different services, leading to incomplete or incorrect trace data. \n\nBut wait, the user hasn\'t checked the dependencies yet. The get_dependency_graph tool could show how the user-service interacts with other services. If there\'s a missing or misconfigured dependency, that might cause the traces to have invalid parent IDs. Also, maybe the user-service is having issues with its own internal operations or with the services it calls. \n\nAlternatively, the problem might be in the Jaeger setup, but since the traces are being collected, that\'s less likely. The next step should be to check the dependency graph to see if there are any anomalies. If the dependency graph shows that the user-service isn\'t properly connected to other services, that could explain the trace issues. \n\nAnother angle: the user-service\'s traces have warnings, but the deployments and pods are all in a healthy state. So the issue isn\'t with the pod\'s health but possibly with the service\'s configuration or how it\'s handling requests. Checking the dependency graph would help identify if there are any broken links or misconfigured services that the user-service is interacting with. \n\nSo, using get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame (like 5 minutes) would provide a visual of the service interactions. If there\'s a service that\'s not properly connected or if there are unexpected dependencies, that could be the root cause. Once the dependency graph is analyzed, if there\'s a problem, the next step would be to check the specific service\'s metrics or logs. But since the user can\'t get logs directly, maybe checking the metrics for the user-service or its dependencies could show any anomalies like high latency or errors.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \n"5"  \nThe traces for the user-service show warnings about invalid parent span IDs, which may indicate issues with service dependencies or tracing configuration. By examining the dependency graph, I can identify if there are missing, misconfigured, or unstable connections between the user-service and other microservices. This will help determine if the root cause lies in faulty dependencies or improper tracing setup.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 576, 'prompt_tokens': 3442, 'total_tokens': 4018, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2b-2348-7742-afc0-02c5316305f6-0', tool_cal