s, then apply it. But since the tool can\'t edit in place, I have to output the full YAML with the changes and use kubectl apply.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use kubectl set to update the probe port and resources. But the tool\'s description says it\'s for safely executing kubectl commands, so perhaps the best way is to generate the updated Deployment YAML and apply it.\n\nSo the plan is:  \n1. Update the livenessProbe port to 9090.  \n2. Add memory limits to the container\'s resources.  \n3. Apply the changes using kubectl apply.  \n\nI need to construct the correct kubectl command for this. Since the Deployment is named user-service in the social-network namespace, the command would be:\n\nkubectl apply -f - <<EOF\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: user-service\n  namespace: social-network\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: user-service\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            port: 9090\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: 512Mi\nEOF\n\nBut I need to make sure the command is correctly formatted. The exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool can run this command. So the tool call should be to execute this kubectl apply command with the modified YAML. That should fix the port issue and add memory limits, preventing OOM kills. After applying, I should wait a bit for the changes to take effect and check if the pod is stable.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: To fix the liveness probe port mismatch and add memory limits, I will update the Deployment\'s container configuration. This will correct the probe to use port `9090` and prevent OOM kills by allocating memory.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: user-service\\n  namespace: social-network\\nspec:\\n  template:\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: user-service\\n        livenessProbe:\\n          httpGet:\\n            port: 9090\\n        resources:\\n          limits:\\n            memory: 512Mi\\nEOF"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 788, 'prompt_tokens': 7042, 'total_tokens': 7830, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2d-42e9-74b1-9d47-e0abf42a437a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7042, 'output_tokens': 788, 'total_tokens': 7830}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c4821910-89d4-4db9-9457-7a6cc7a33ebb'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me go through the steps here. The user is trying to fix the user-service pod that's crashing. From the previous interactions, we've identified two main issues: the liveness probe is checking the wrong port (8080 instead of 9090), and the pod is running out of memory, leading to OOM kills.\n\nFirst, the liveness probe configuration in the Deployment is set to port 8080, but the container actually listens on port 9090. This causes the probe to fail, triggering restarts. The solution is to update the livenessProbe's httpGet port to 9090.\n\nSecond, the resources section in the container's spec is empty. Adding memory limits here will prevent the pod from being killed due to memory exhaustion. The user mentioned an exit code 137, which is typical for an OOM kill, so setting a memory limit like 512Mi should help.\n\nThe next step is to apply these changes. The user used the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to apply the updated Deployment configuration. The command they used correctly modifies the livenessProbe's port and adds memory limits. \n\nAfter applying the changes, it's important to wait for the deployment to rollout and the pod to restart. The wait_tool is used here to pause for 60 seconds, giving the cluster time to process the update and for the new pod to come up. \n\nFinally, verifying the fix involves checking the pod's status to ensure it's running without crashes and confirming that the liveness probe is now using the correct port. If everything is successful, the mitigation is complete.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: user-service\\n  namespace: social-network\\nspec:\\n  template:\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: user-service\\n        livenessProbe:\\n          httpGet:\\n            port: 9090\\n        resources:\\n          limits:\\n            memory: 512Mi\\nEOF"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-990e0c51ebba7a1a', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 463, 'prompt_tokens': 8253, 'total_tokens': 8716, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2d-693c-7603-95a6-410bdfa1edc0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: user-service\n  namespace: social-network\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: user-service\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            port: 9090\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: 512Mi\nEOF'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-990e0c51ebba7a1a', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8253, 'output_tokens': 463, 'total_tokens': 8716}), ToolMess