ould be to correct the probe port and adjust memory limits. But first, I need to inspect the current configuration to identify the exact issues.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: To diagnose the crash, I need to inspect the user-service pod\'s YAML configuration to verify the liveness probe settings (port 8080 vs actual container port 9090) and resource limits (memory constraints causing OOM kills). This will confirm the root cause before applying fixes.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl get pod -n social-network user-service-94d6f89b9-c99rw -o yaml"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 388, 'prompt_tokens': 3408, 'total_tokens': 3796, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2c-d152-7cb2-9fc3-1e47eaac37b8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3408, 'output_tokens': 388, 'total_tokens': 3796}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6b77f121-88e7-4cb7-9dc7-1a558f92f5a6'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me figure out what to do next. The user is dealing with a crashing user-service pod in their Kubernetes cluster. From the previous steps, they\'ve already listed all the pods and noticed that user-service is in CrashLoopBackOff. They tried getting the pod\'s YAML but maybe didn\'t get the full details yet.\n\nWait, in the last tool call, the user used "kubectl get pods -n social-network" and saw the user-service pod is crashing. Then they tried to get the YAML for that specific pod. The response from the tool might not have been fully processed yet. The next step should be to check the pod\'s configuration to find out why it\'s crashing. \n\nThe user needs to look into the liveness probe settings. Earlier, they mentioned that the liveness probe was checking port 8080, but the container is actually listening on 9090. That\'s a common issue. Also, the exit code 137 indicates an OOM kill, so memory limits might be too low.\n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the detailed YAML of the user-service pod to check the liveness probe configuration and resource limits. The command would be "kubectl get pod -n social-network user-service-<pod-name> -o yaml". But since the pod name might have changed, using the exact name from the previous output, like "user-service-94d6f89b9-c99rw", is necessary. \n\nWait, the user already tried that in the previous step. The tool response might not have been fully captured. Maybe they need to check the pod\'s description or logs next. Alternatively, they could check the deployment\'s YAML to see the probe settings. But since the pod is crashing, checking the pod\'s own configuration is more direct. \n\nAlternatively, using "kubectl describe pod" could provide more details about the restarts and events leading to the crash. But the user\'s focus is on the liveness probe and resource limits, so getting the YAML is still the right move. \n\nSo, the correct tool call is to execute "kubectl get pod -n social-network user-service-94d6f89b9-c99rw -o yaml" again to retrieve the full configuration and verify the probe settings and resource limits.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pod -n social-network user-service-94d6f89b9-c99rw -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a5679f5a0918504b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 529, 'prompt_tokens': 4567, 'total_tokens': 5096, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prom