      httpGet:\\n            port: 9090\\n        resources:\\n          limits:\\n            memory: 512Mi\\nEOF"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-919bae8e95e1771c', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 427, 'prompt_tokens': 8748, 'total_tokens': 9175, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2d-a15b-70f2-92f7-f4ad204a05a0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: user-service\n  namespace: social-network\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      service: user-service\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        service: user-service\n        app: user-service\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: user-service\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            port: 9090\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: 512Mi\nEOF'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-919bae8e95e1771c', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8748, 'output_tokens': 427, 'total_tokens': 9175}), ToolMessage(content='Dry-run failed. Potentially it\'s an invalid command. stderr: The Deployment "user-service" is invalid: spec.template.spec.containers[0].image: Required value\n', id='b0c8bd8f-37eb-45f0-8737-f7243c49ec8f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-919bae8e95e1771c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='94dac0c3-7fbb-4f0a-9ece-aaada4277627'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going wrong here. The user is trying to update the Deployment for the user-service in their Kubernetes cluster. They\'ve had several attempts to apply changes, but each time there\'s an error. The latest error message says: "The Deployment \'user-service\' is invalid: spec.template.spec.containers[0].image: Required value". \n\nHmm, that error indicates that the image field is missing in the container specification. Looking back at the previous tool calls, the user\'s patch didn\'t include the image field. In the original Deployment YAML they retrieved earlier, the container had an image specified as "docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest". \n\nSo, when they tried to apply the patch, they only modified the livenessProbe port and added memory limits, but omitted the image field. Kubernetes requires that the image be present in the container spec. Therefore, the correct approach is to include the image in the patch to ensure the container is properly defined.\n\nThe user needs to update the Deployment with the correct image, set the liveness probe port to 9090, and add memory limits. They also need to make sure that the selector and labels are correctly synchronized to avoid the earlier errors. By including the image in the patch, the Deployment should be valid and apply without errors.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The previous attempt failed because the container\'s `image` field was missing. I must include the original image definition to ensure the Deployment is valid. The final patch will:  \n1. Preserve the original image (`docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest`).  \n2. Set the liveness probe port to `9090`.  \n3. Add memory limits (`512Mi`).  \n4. Maintain selector/label synchronization.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: user-service\\n  namespace: social-network\\nspec:\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      service: user-service\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        service: user-service\\n        app: user-service\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: user-service\\n        image: docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\\n        livenessProbe:\\n          httpGet:\\n            port: 9090\\n        resources:\\n          limits:\\n            memory: 512Mi\\nEOF"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 566, 'prompt_tokens': 7971, 'total_tokens': 8537, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2d-bb20-7032-8447-ec6a91b377ee-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7971, 'output_tokens': 566, 'total_tokens': 8537}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e4bd7346-64f5-43f3-a36a-06ab8558083a'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to fix the user-service pod in their Kubernetes cluster. From the previous steps, they've already checked the pod and deployment configurations. They found that the liveness probe is set to port 8080, but the container is actually listening on 9090. Also, there's no memory limit, leading to OOM kills.\n\nThe user tried applying a patch to update the deployment, but there were errors. The first error was about the selector and labels not matching. Then, when they tried including the image, there was another error. Now, they need to make sure the patch includes all necessary fields without causing validation errors.\n\nLooking at the last tool call, the command includes the image, liveness probe port, and memory limits. But maybe the issue is that the original deployment has more fields that need to be preserved. For example, the container might have other configurations like command, ports, or volume mounts that were omitted in the patch. If those are missing, Kubernetes might reject the deployment because it's incomplete.\n\nSo, the correct approach is to ensure that the patch includes all the necessary fields from the original deployment. The user should copy the existing container configuration, modify only the required parts (liveness probe port and memory limits), and then apply the patch. This way, the deployment remains valid and all required fields are present.\n\nTherefore, the next step is to generate a tool call that applies the patch with the correct image, liveness probe settings, memory limits, and all other necessary container configurations. This should resolve the validation errors and fix the pod's issues.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: user-service\\n  namespace: social-network\\nspec:\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      service: user-service\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        service: user-service\\n        app: user-service\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: user-service\\n        image: docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\\n        command:\\n        - UserService\\n        ports:\\n        - containerPort: 9090\\n          protocol: TCP\\n        livenessProbe:\\n          httpGet:\\n            port: 9090\\n            path: /healthz\\n        resources:\\n          limits:\\n            memory: 512Mi\\n        volumeMounts:\\n        - mountPath: /social-network-microservices/config/jaeger-config.yml\\n          name: user-service-config\\n          subPath: jaeger-config.yml\\n        - mountPath: /social-network-microservices/config/service-config.json\\n          name: user-service-config\\n          subPath: service-config.json\\nEOF"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98959f4a64c4e7ca', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 619, 'prompt_tokens': 9282, 'total_tokens': 9901, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2d-d69f-7313-9fb8-5dbf08115535-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: user-service\n  namespace: social-network\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      service: user-service\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        service: user-service\n        app: user-service\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: user-service\n        image: docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\n        command:\n        - UserService\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 9090\n          protocol: TCP\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            port: 9090\n            path: /healthz\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: 512Mi\n        volumeMounts:\n        - mountPath: /social-network-microservices/config/jaeger-config.yml\n          name: user-service-config\n          subPath: jaeger-config.yml\n        - mountPath: /social-network-microservices/config/service-config.json\n          name: user-service-config\n          subPath: service-config.json\nEOF'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98959f4a64c4e7ca', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9282, 'output_tokens': 619, 'total_tokens': 9901}), ToolMessage(content='Dry-run failed. Potentially it\'s an invalid command. stderr: The Deployment "user-service" is invalid: \n* spec.template.spec.co