ce-c85b5c7f6-9g2vv       1/1     Running   0          6m40s\nuser-memcached-59967f9b78-fwgpg           1/1     Running   0          6m40s\nuser-mention-service-d8cbd6fcf-hvff4      1/1     Running   0          6m38s\nuser-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-kcvfc             1/1     Running   0          6m40s\nuser-service-779977866b-5hnfv             1/1     Running   0          21s\nuser-timeline-mongodb-6ccb4447d8-tkfdz    1/1     Running   0          6m40s\nuser-timeline-redis-546bbd644f-l2xwm      1/1     Running   0          6m40s\nuser-timeline-service-6479b8f4fd-2x2j5    1/1     Running   0          6m39s\nwrk2-job-wqzsx                            1/1     Running   0          5m8s\n', id='ecd33761-d6ff-4aef-8182-d4b251df7ceb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bb84f7a7ad22b189'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6220e74f-4aa8-4c5d-80f4-0e8f676b3348'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the user-service pod that was crashing. They applied a Deployment update to correct the liveness probe port and add memory limits. Now, they need to verify if the fix worked.\n\nFirst, they checked the pod status but got an error because the pod name changed after the Deployment update. So, they listed all the pods in the namespace and found the new pod name: user-service-779977866b-5hnfv. It\'s showing as Running with 1/1 containers ready. That\'s a good sign.\n\nBut to be thorough, they should check the pod\'s logs to ensure there are no errors. The logs can confirm if the liveness probe is working correctly and if the memory limits are preventing OOM kills. Using kubectl logs to check the latest logs would help verify the fix. If the logs show the probe is succeeding and no memory issues, then the problem is resolved. If not, they might need to adjust the memory limits further or check other configurations. But based on the current status, the pod is running, so the fix is likely effective.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The new pod `user-service-779977866b-5hnfv` is running, but I need to verify if the liveness probe is functioning and there are no lingering memory issues. Checking the pod\'s logs will confirm stability.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl logs -n social-network user-service-779977866b-5hnfv"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 364, 'prompt_tokens': 10848, 'total_tokens': 11212, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2e-9aa6-7070-9997-7993b0eda31f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10848, 'output_tokens': 364, 'total_tokens': 11212}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4866e646-5b8e-467d-b906-3158841aa720'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to fix the user-service pod in their Kubernetes cluster. They've gone through several steps, updating the Deployment to correct the liveness probe port and add memory limits. Now, after applying the changes, they want to check if the pod is stable.\n\nFirst, they tried to get the status of the specific pod, but it wasn't found. That makes sense because the pod name changed after the Deployment update. So they listed all the pods in the namespace and found the new pod name: user-service-779977866b-5hnfv. \n\nNow, they're checking the logs of this new pod to ensure everything is working. The logs should show if the liveness probe is succeeding and if there are any memory-related issues. If the logs are clean and the pod is running without restarts, then the problem is likely resolved. \n\nBut wait, the user might also want to confirm that the memory limits are effective. Maybe they should check the resource usage metrics to ensure the pod isn't hitting the memory limits again. However, the available tools include get_metrics from Prometheus, which could be used to query memory usage. But since the user hasn't mentioned that yet, maybe they just need to rely on the logs and the pod's current status.\n\nThe next step after checking the logs would be to verify the pod's status again. If the logs are good and the pod is in the Running state, then the issue is fixed. If there are still errors, they might need to adjust the memory limits further or check other configurations. But based on the previous steps, the main issues were the port and memory, so if those are fixed, the pod should be stable.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs -n social-network user-service-779977866b-5hnfv"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bc4972bb335a024d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 413, 'prompt_tokens': 11997, 'total_tokens': 12410, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2e-ad2c-7d72-919c-ed576432edaa-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs -n social-network user-service-779977866b-5hnfv'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bc4972bb335a024d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11997, 'output_tokens': 413, 'total_tokens': 12410}), ToolMessage(content='[2026-Mar-17 14:23:43.147959] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = c6:52:ca:a2:81:42\n[2026-Mar-17 14:23:43.148266] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = 842\n[2026-Mar-17 14:23:43.154693] <info>: (UserService.cpp:94:main) Starting the user-service server ...\nThrift: Tue Mar 17 14:23:48 2026 TConnectedClient died: Received an oversized frame\nThrift: Tue Mar 17 14:23:58 2026 TConnectedClient died: Received an oversized frame\nThrift: Tue Mar 17 14:24:08 2026 TConnectedClient died: Received an oversized frame\n', id='dabff8b0-5d39-42c3-ae3c-29206828236a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bc4972bb335a024d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1b92161f-2fd7-453b-aca6-9e6f662acb1b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user-service pod is running now, but there are some Thrift-related errors in the logs. The logs show "Received an oversized frame" multiple times. That might indicate a problem with the Thrift RPC communication between services. \n\nFirst, I need to check if the service is properly configured. The previous steps fixed the liveness probe and memory issues, so the pod isn\'t crashing anymore. But the Thrift errors suggest there\'s a problem with the service\'s network settings or the way it\'s handling incoming requests.\n\nMaybe the service is using the wrong port or there\'s a misconfiguration in the Thrift server settings. The user-service is supposed to listen on port 9090, which was corrected in the Deployment. But the Thrift errors could be due to the client or server not correctly handling the frame size. \n\nI should check the service\'s YAML to ensure the port is correctly exposed. Also, looking at the service\'s configuration in the namespace might help. The service might be using a ClusterIP, but if there\'s a misconfiguration, it could cause issues with how clients connect. \n\nAnother possibility is that the Thrift server has a limit on the frame size, and the incoming requests are exceeding that. The logs mention "oversized frame," so adjusting the Thrift server\'s frame size settings might be necessary. \n\nI should also check the network policies to ensure there are no restrictions on the ports or traffic between services. If there\'s a NetworkPolicy blocking the traffic, that could cause the Thrift client to fail. \n\nAlternatively, the service might not be properly registered with the service discovery, leading to clients connecting to the wrong endpoints. Checking the service\'s endpoints and ensuring they match the pods\' IPs and ports would be important. \n\nSince the user-service is part of a microservices architecture using Thrift RPCs, ensuring that all services are correctly configured to communicate with each other is crucial. The next step would be to verify the service\'s configuration, check the Thrift server settings, and ensure network policies are not blocking the traffic. If all else fails, checking the service\'s logs for more detailed errors could provide more insight.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_services  \nreasoning: To ensure the user-service is correctly exposed and reachable via its service, I need to verify the service configuration (e.g., port mapping, selector, and type). This will confirm if the service is properly routing traffic to the updated pod.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "get_services",\n  "args": {}\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 533, 'prompt_tokens': 11324, 'total_tokens': 11857, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2e-c559-7483-9c42-569efb55f4bc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11324, 'output_tokens': 533, 'total_tokens': 11857}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='10f0fb13-44ca-4395-a7a3-cd855b54bab2'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to fix the user-service pod in their social network application. They've already gone through several steps: checking the pod's YAML, updating the Deployment to fix the liveness probe port and add memory limits, and verifying the new pod's status. Now, they're seeing some Thrift errors in the logs about oversized frames. \n\nFirst, I need to recall the previous steps. The initial issue was the pod crashing due to a misconfigured liveness probe (port 8080 vs 9090) and OOM kills. The user fixed the probe port and added memory limits, which should have resolved those issues. However, the logs now show Thrift errors, which might indicate a different problem, possibly related to network configuration or service exposure.\n\nThe user's last action was checking the logs, which showed Thrift errors. The next step they took was to call the get_services tool to verify the service configuration. The get_services tool retrieves the list of services from Grafana. But the user hasn't seen the results yet. \n\nWait, the user's last tool call was get_services, and the response would be a list of service names. The user needs to check if the user-service's corresponding service (like user-service) is correctly configured. For example, ensuring that the service's selector matches the pod's labels, the ports are correctly mapped, and the service type is appropriate (ClusterIP, NodePort, etc.). \n\nIf the service isn't properly configured, even if the pod is running, the service might not be routing traffic correctly, leading to issues like the Thrift errors. The Thrift errors could be due to the service not being accessible, causing clients to timeout or receive oversized frames. \n\nSo, the next step after getting the list of services is to check the specific service configuration for user-service. The user might need to use get_operations or get_metrics to see if there are any issues with the service's endpoints or traffic. Alternatively, checking the service's YAML with kubectl to ensure the ports and selectors are correct. \n\nBut since the user has already fixed the Deployment, the next logical step is to verify the service configuration. If the service is misconfigured, the pod might be running but not accessible, leading to the Thrift errors. Therefore, the user should first get the list of services to see if user-service is present and then inspect its details.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b744dc56a31f4102', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 524, 'prompt_tokens': 12426, 'total_tokens': 12950, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2e-dff9-77c3-bedc-3b1e9ee7887d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b744dc56a31f4102', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12426, 'output_tokens': 524, 'total_tokens': 12950}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'user-service\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='b05cdfcb-7d93-4eee-8f5f-3df2944ec024', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b744dc56a31f4102'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c13418d2-1795-43d3-81bf-fec20142775e')]]
2026-03-17 14:24:30 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 11] Inject tool call prompt (repeated) - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_prompt_inject_step:85
2026-03-17 14:24:34 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 11] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='af1f128d-2c25-48b6-9c21-825cad73c03d'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\n\nSocial Network\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA social network with unidirectional follow relationships, implemented with loosely-coupled microservices, communicating with each other via Thrift RPCs.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nsocial-network\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with a social network application made up of microservices, and they're trying to diagnose why the user-service pod is crashing. From the conversation history, they've gone through several steps:\n\n1. **Initial Checks**: They started by listing all services, checking deployments, and pods. Everything seemed healthy at first glance.\n2. **Traces and Metrics**: They looked into traces and found warnings about invalid parent span IDs. Metrics queries didn't return data, which was confusing.\n3. **Service Configuration**: They checked the service's YAML and found that the endpoints were empty, which suggested a problem with the service selector.\n4. **Pod Logs**: The pod was in CrashLoopBackOff, and the logs showed some warnings but no clear error. The describe pod command revealed that the liveness probe was failing because it was checking port 8080, but the container was listening on 9090.\n5. **Root Cause**: The main issues were the misconfigured liveness probe port and insufficient memory allocation leading to OOM kills.\n\nSo, the final answer needs to summarize these findings. The user-service pod is crashing because the liveness probe is set to check the wrong port (8080 instead of 9090), causing the probe to fail and triggering restarts. Additionally, the pod is running out of memory, as indicated by the exit code 137 and memory-related warnings in the logs. The solution would involve correcting the liveness probe port to 9090 and adjusting the memory limits in the pod's configuration to prevent OOM kills.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the user-service pod failure is a combination of two critical issues:\n\n1. **Misconfigured Liveness Probe**: The liveness probe is incorrectly configured to check port `8080`, but the container only listens on port `9090`. This causes the probe to fail repeatedly, triggering continuous restarts (`CrashLoopBackOff`).\n\n2. **Out-of-Memory (OOM) Exhaustion**: The pod is crashing with exit code `137`, indicating it was killed due to insufficient memory. Logs show warnings about memory allocation (e.g., falling back to `malloc` for counters), confirming resource constraints.\n\n**Solution**:  \n- Correct the liveness probe to use port `9090` in the pod's configuration.  \n- Adjust memory limits in the pod's resource specifications to prevent OOM kills.  \n\nThese changes will resolve the probe failures and resource exhaustion, stabilizing the user-service pod.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='fa4e1cc1-d436-455a-b6f0-523f80b694f8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4347ccdc-4356-4e2a-ae24-bcc8d68b06c4'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what the next step should be. The user is dealing with a social network application where the user-service pod is crashing. From the previous conversation, they\'ve already identified some issues, like the liveness probe checking the wrong port and memory problems. But they need to confirm these findings and apply the fixes.\n\nFirst, I need to check if the liveness probe is indeed configured incorrectly. The user mentioned that the pod\'s liveness probe was set to port 8080, but the container listens on 9090. To verify this, I should look at the pod\'s configuration. The `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` tool can be used to get the pod\'s YAML definition. That way, I can confirm the probe settings and the container\'s port.\n\nOnce I have the YAML, I can check the `livenessProbe` section. If the port is wrong, I\'ll need to correct it. Also, checking the resource limits (like memory) in the pod\'s spec will help confirm if the OOM issue is due to insufficient memory allocation.\n\nSo the next logical step is to retrieve the pod\'s configuration using `kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml` via the `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` tool. This will provide the necessary details to validate the root causes identified earlier and proceed with the fixes.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: To confirm the liveness probe configuration and resource limits causing the crash, I need to inspect the user-service pod\'s YAML definition. This will reveal the incorrect port in the liveness probe and verify memory constraints.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "args": {\n    "command": "kubectl get pod -n social-network user-service-<pod-name> -o yaml"\n  }\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 394, 'prompt_tokens': 2215, 'total_tokens': 2609, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2c-a99c-7862-9707-9bb5cadf36ac-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2215, 'output_tokens': 394, 'total_tokens': 2609}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='884f6371-d369-40b9-a92a-d2ed47dc91a2'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me figure out the next step. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service pod is crashing. Earlier, they checked the pod\'s logs and found that the liveness probe was failing because it was checking port 8080, but the container was listening on 9090. They also noticed memory issues leading to OOM kills.\n\nNow, the assistant chose the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the pod\'s YAML definition. The command they used was "kubectl get pod -n social-network user-service-<pod-name> -o yaml". But wait, the pod name has a placeholder <pod-name>. That\'s a problem because the actual pod name isn\'t specified. The user needs to replace that with the real pod name to get the correct configuration.\n\nSo, the next step should be to first retrieve the list of pods in the social-network namespace to find the exact name of the user-service pod. That way, they can use the correct pod name in the subsequent kubectl command. Without the correct pod name, the YAML retrieval won\'t work as intended. Let me check the available tools. There\'s a get_services tool, but maybe the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely can be used to list the pods. Alternatively, using kubectl get pods in the namespace would help. Wait, the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool can execute any kubectl command, so the assistant should first run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to list all pods and identify the user-service pod\'s name. Once they have the name, they can then retrieve the YAML for that specific pod. That makes sense. So the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the namespace to find the correct pod name.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae17c797d36bd8fa', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 396, 'prompt_tokens': 3346, 'total_tokens': 3742, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc2c-bc06-7e10-8afb-a29fa52d8dd2-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae17c797d36bd8fa', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3346, 'output_tokens': 396, 'total_tokens': 3742}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS             RESTARTS      AGE\ncompose-post-service-886c98b48-bxtrx      1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nhome-timeline-redis-5cdbd67f7c-kt46k      1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nhome-timeline-service-5d6bb5597d-qwvp5    1/1     Running            0             4m41s\njaeger-68c668dfbc-lvg27                   1/1     Running            0             4m40s\nmedia-frontend-f49684d4c-hgs45            1/1     Running            0             4m40s\nmedia-memcached-8bdff88f6-6mfmt           1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nmedia-mongodb-85f4f7f55-bn8f8             1/1     Running            0             4m42s\nmedia-service-bb9d6c8d6-rzw5r             1/1     Running            0             4m42s\nnginx-thrift-867cc56796-rdc9m             1/1     Running            0             4m40s\npost-storage-memcached-55dcdfffbd-lpxlh   1/1     Running            0             4m43s\npost-storage-mongodb-5bf878564c-jdtfh     1/1     Running            0             4m41s\npost-storage-service-76b64fb6dc-g7bq8     1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nsocial-graph-mongodb-596cdcf88c-9tm5q     1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nsocial-graph-redis-69c658d694-zq9mj       1/1     Running            0             4m42s\nsocial-graph-service-79c848748f-2w6fx     1/1     Running            0             4m43s\ntext-service-6745755f75-8mtsr             1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nunique-id-service-5578bf47d8-xmlck        1/1     Running            0             4m41s\nurl-shorten-memcached-64c6c7557d-9nbrn    1/1     Running            0             4m42s\nurl-shorten-mongodb-ccd7bb58d-vpl95       1/1     Running            0             4m43s\nurl-short